<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isa ibn Ali Al Khalifa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a>–<a href="/years/1932">1932</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isa ibn Ali Al <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalifa">Khalifa</a> was ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> from 1869, when the British installed him after a period of civil war, until 1923. His reign saw Bahrain bound to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> by the exclusive agreements of 1880 and 1892. In 1923 the British, pressing for administrative reform, forced him to hand power to his son Hamad, though he kept the title until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isa-ibn-ali-al-khalifa.jpg" alt="Isa ibn Ali Al Khalifa" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous photographer between 1860-1923 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isa_bin_Ali_Al_Khalifa_(1848_%E2%80%93_1932).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Isa bin Ali Al Khalifa was born on 27 November 1848 at Riffa Fort, the fourth son of Sheikh Ali bin Khalifa Al Khalifa. His father became ruler in 1868, after the British forced his brother Muhammad bin Khalifa Al Khalifa to abdicate over an alleged breach of the 1861 treaty against maritime depredations. In August 1869 a large force led by Nasir bin Mubarak invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> and killed Sheikh Ali, and Nasir's cousin Mohammed bin Abdullah seized the throne. Isa fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zubarah">Zubarah</a>, where the Al Noaim tribe remained loyal to him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In December 1869 a British force under the Political Resident Lewis Pelly deposed the usurper, who died a captive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1877, and Isa ruled from 2 December 1869. A treaty of 22 December 1880 bound him to make no treaty or engagement with any other foreign power without British consent. On his brother's death in October 1888 he became sole ruler, his title changing from Chief to Ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, or Hakim. The treaty was confirmed and extended on 13 March 1892, Isa keeping for himself the management of Bahrain's internal affairs.</p><p>With his brother Sheikh Khalid bin Ali he fought off an attempted invasion by a maritime force of the Al Binali tribe in 1895. In 1923 the British political adviser Clive Kirkpatrick Daly forced him to abdicate, but Bahrainis never recognised the abdication and regarded his son Hamad only as vice-ruler. From 1926 the British adviser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-belgrave">Charles Belgrave</a> helped carry through administrative reforms, among them laws to regulate pearl fishing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Isa died while saying the dawn prayers in his room in Muharraq on 9 December 1932, having held the title of ruler for 63 years, the longest such span in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>'s history, and was buried at the Muharraq cemetery. He was officially succeeded by his second son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamad-bin-isa-al-khalifa">Hamad bin Isa Al Khalifa</a>, his eldest son Salman having died in 1893 on the way back from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a>. Among his honours were the Order of the Star of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1914 and a knighthood of the Order of the Indian Empire in 1919.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Isa bin Ali Al Khalifa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Britain forced <b>Isa bin Ali</b> to hand power to his son Hamad in 1923, Hamad and the British adviser Charles Belgrave, from 1926, created courts, schools and a modern administration.</blockquote><a href="/state/sheikhdom-of-bahrain">Sheikhdom of Bahrain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manama">Manama</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bahrain">Bahrain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.07,50.55&amp;year=1902" aria-label="Bahrain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5885 1206 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Isa ibn Ali Al Khalifa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6335 1476h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6335 1476h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=26.07,50.55&amp;year=1902">What was here in 1902? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
